  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 110 PM CDT Tue Jul 21 2026\n\nThe remainder of the day will remain relatively dry for a \nmajority of the area, with isolated chances for showers mainly \nacross southern and northeastern portions of the area. High\nresolution guidance continues to suggest the possibility of a few \npopup showers from as an embedded shortwave in the long wave troughing\nto our north allows for convection to drift into our area, while \nthe influence of Tropical Storm Bertha, drifting northwest just \noff the Gulf Coast, is felt across our south. This is expected to\nremain sub-severe but a few strong storms aren't out of the\nquestion.\n\nDangerous heat stress conditions will continue across the area \nthrough Wednesday, with the focus shifting south of I-20\nWednesday. This will be reflected in the GHWO by an elevated risk\nof heat related stress conditions areawide with areas along and \nsouth of I-20 indicated as having a significant risk for heat \nrelated stress conditions. In addition, a mix of Excessive Heat \nWarnings and Heat Advisories will cover the 105+ heat index values\nthrough then. By Thursday, rain chances and the proximity of the \nfrontal boundary in the region make hazardous heat less certain \nfor Thursday or later at this time. \n\nTropical Storm Bertha, a sheared storm, will \ncontinue to move slowly west along the Gulf Coast over the next \nfew days. No significant impacts are expected in our forecast area\nas it passes to the south, but winds will be a little breezy from\nthe north Wednesday into Thursday.\n\nA robust upper-level ridge centered over the Southern High Plains\nis reinforcing the dry, hot conditions over our region meanwhile.\nA cool front sagging into the region by Wednesday will serve as a\nfocus for afternoon shower and thunderstorm development for the\nback half of the week and next weekend./NF/KP/",
